Native replacements of IOP module functions (plan P5, the readable port of YYSOUND). ps2x/iop/iop_native.h: NativeContext, the interface a replacement sees (registers, IOP RAM as host memory, callGuest for imports and unported functions, jump for a tail call such as a thread body's SleepThread, charge for IOP time). IopSubsystem::registerNativeModule and registerNative: functions are registered by offset in a module's image and take effect only when the module loads with the image whose hash was registered. IopEmulator: a table checked in step() next to the import stubs; PS2X_IOP_NATIVE=0|names turns replacements off or selects them; PS2X_IOP_VERIFY=1 runs the original first (its imports happen once and are logged with the RAM they change), rolls RAM back, runs the replacement with the module's imports answered from the log, and compares RAM (the dead stack and argument home area excepted), the registers the ABI keeps, the import sequence and the return, then continues from the original's state; PS2X_IOP_DUMP_MODULES=<dir> writes the loaded image of a module with replacements. IopMemory::ramData and PS2Runtime::iopSubsystem give the port access. Blocking: a function registered with mayBlock runs on a host fiber (one per IOP thread); when guest code it called puts the thread to sleep, callFunction parks the fiber with the function's C++ frames intact, the scheduler runs other threads and, when the thread is ready again, switches back into the fiber, which carries on from where the guest code slept. The call stacks of callFunction are taken from a pool (callSlots) instead of by depth, so a parked call keeps its stack. A function that ends by jumping to an import stub (a thread body going to SleepThread or sceSifRpcLoop) gives its parkStub, which is where the verifier compares it. currentNativeContext() gives the context of the running native, kept right across nested natives and fiber switches. IopKernel::currentThread gives the running thread. Test aids of the verifier: PS2X_IOP_COVERAGE (the words of the module the original executed in verified calls), PS2X_IOP_COVERAGE_ALL (every word that runs), PS2X_IOP_PROBE (commands injected into the sound function) with PS2X_IOP_VERIFY_ALL, PS2X_IOP_TRACE_NATIVE (calls logged) and PS2X_IOP_DUMP_IMPORTS (the original's imports at a mismatch). Import effects replayed in whole words; the instructions run inside the imports are counted so that a replacement's cost excludes them; IopSubsystem::registerVerifyIgnore leaves given RAM out of the comparison after a call of a named replacement.

diff --git a/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_native.h b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_native.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ps2xIOP/include/ps2x/iop/iop_native.h
@@ -0,0 +1,44 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <cstdint>
+
+namespace ps2x::iop
+{
+    // What a native (host C++) replacement of an IOP function sees and may do. The emulator calls the replacement when the IOP reaches
+    // the function's address, with the caller's registers as they are on entry (a0 to a3 are registers 4 to 7); the function ends by
+    // returning to the caller (v0, register 2, is the result) unless it calls jump().
+    class NativeContext
+    {
+    public:
+        virtual ~NativeContext() = default;
+
+        // The 2 MB of IOP RAM as host memory; an IOP address is masked to its physical part (address & 0x1FFFFF).
+        virtual uint8_t *ram() = 0;
+        virtual uint32_t reg(unsigned index) const = 0;
+        virtual void setReg(unsigned index, uint32_t value) = 0;
+
+        // Runs guest code to its return and gives v0: an import stub, or a function of the module that is not replaced. The code runs on
+        // its own stack, as the emulator's other synchronous calls do.
+        virtual uint32_t callGuest(uint32_t address, uint32_t a0 = 0, uint32_t a1 = 0, uint32_t a2 = 0, uint32_t a3 = 0) = 0;
+
+        // How many of a0 to a3 the next callGuest really passes (an import takes fewer than four: the rest are leftovers in the original, which a
+        // replacement cannot reproduce, so the verifier does not compare them).
+        virtual void argumentCount(unsigned count) = 0;
+
+        // The arguments after the fourth of the next callGuest (the caller's stack, at sp + 16, + 20, + 24 as in the o32 convention).
+        virtual void stackArguments(const uint32_t *values, unsigned count) = 0;
+
+        // Ends the function by continuing in guest code at `address` with the registers as they are now (a tail call): how a thread body
+        // sleeps (ra = where it resumes, address = the SleepThread import stub).
+        virtual void jump(uint32_t address) = 0;
+
+        // Instructions the original would have run; the IOP's time moves on by this much, so timers and DMA completions keep their schedule.
+        virtual void charge(uint32_t instructions) = 0;
+    };
+
+    using NativeFunction = void (*)(NativeContext &);
+
+    // The context of the native function that is running now (null when none). The emulator keeps it right when a function calls guest code
+    // that runs another native, and when a function that went to sleep (see IopSubsystem::registerNative, mayBlock) is resumed.
+    NativeContext *currentNativeContext() noexcept;
+}
